"1. BMJ Paediatr Open. 2024 Oct 30;8(1):e002833. doi: 10.1136/bmjpo-2024-002833.\n\nAntibiotic prescribing practices according to the AWaRe classification among \nchildren under 5 of age attending public primary care centres in four West \nAfrican countries: a cross-sectional study (AIRE project, 2021-2022).\n\nGres E(1)(2), Diallo IS(3), Besnier C(2), Diakit\u00e9 AA(4), Zair Z(2), Ou\u00e9draogo \nYugbar\u00e9 S(5), Hedible GB(2), Sawadogo AG(6), Kargougou D(7), Koli\u00e9 JS(8), Meda \nB(9), Busi\u00e8re S(10), Lamontagne F(11), Ridde V(12), Leroy V(2); AIRE Research \nStudy Group.\n\nCollaborators: Yugbar\u00e9 Ou\u00e9draogo S, Sanon Zombr\u00e9 VM, Sama Cherif M, Diallo IS, \nKaba DF, Diakit\u00e9 AA, Sidib\u00e9 A, Abarry Souleymane H, Tidjani IssaganaDikouma F, \nAgbeci H, Catala L, Dahourou DL, Desmonde S, Gres E, Hedible GB, Leroy V, Peters \nBokol L, Tavarez J, Zair Z, Louart S, Ridde V, Cousien A, Becquet R, Briand V, \nJournot V, Lenaud S, N'Chot C, Seri B, Yao C, Anago G, Badiane D, Kinda M, \nNeboua D, Dia PS, Shepherd S, di Mauro N, No\u00ebl G, Nyoka K, Taokreo W, Coulidiati \nLompo OB, Vignon M, Aba P, Diallo N, Ngaradoum M, L\u00e9no S, Sow AT, Bald\u00e9 A, \nSoumah A, Bald\u00e9 B, Bah F, Millimouno KC, Haba M, Bah M, Soumah M, Guilavogui M, \nSylla MN, Diallo S, Dounfangadouno SF, Bah TI, Sani S, Gnongoue C, Gaye S, \nGuilavogui J, Tour\u00e9 AO, Koli\u00e9 JS, Savadogo AS, Sangala F, Traore M, Konare T, \nCoulibaly A, Keita A, Diarra D, Traor\u00e9 H, Sangar\u00e9 I, Kon\u00e9 I, Traor\u00e9 M, Diarra S, \nOpoue V, Keita FK, Dougabka M, Demb\u00e9l\u00e9 B, Doumbia MS, Kargougou GD, Keita S, \nBouille S, Calmettes S, Lamontagne F, Harouna KH, Moutari B, Issaka I, Assoumane \nSO, Dioiri S, Sidi M, Sani Alio K, Amina S, Agbokou R, Hamidou MG, Sani SM, \nMahamane A, Abdou A, Ousmane B, Kabirou I, Mahaman I, Mamoudou I, Baguido M, \nAbdoul R, Sahabi A, Seini F, Hamani Z, Meda LB, Niome M, Toviho X, Sanouna I, \nKouam P, Busi\u00e8re S, Triclin F, Hema A, Bayala M, Tapsoba L, Yaro JB, Sougue S, \nBakyono R, Sawadogo AG, Soumah A, Lompo YA, Malgoubri B, Douamba F, Sore G, \nWangraoua L, Yamponi S, Bayala SI, Tiegna S, Kam S, Yoda S, Karantao M, Barry \nDF, Sanou O, Nacoulma N, Semde N, Ouattara I, Wango F, Gneissien Z, Congo H, \nDiarra Y, Ouattara B, Maiga A, Diabate F, Goita O, Gana S, Diallo S, Sylla S, \nCoulibaly D, Sakho N, Kadio K, Yougbar\u00e9 J, Zongo D, Tougouma S, Dicko A, Nanema \nZ, Balima I, Ouedraogo A, Ouattara A, Coulibaly SE, Bald\u00e9 H, Barry L, Duparc \nHaba E, Coulibaly A, Sidibe T, Sangare Y, Traore B, Diarra Y, Dagobi AE, Salifou \nS, Gana MoustaphaCh\u00e9tima B, Abdou IH.\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)National Institute for Health and Medical Research (INSERM) UMR 1219, \nResearch Institute for Sustainable Development (IRD), Bordeaux Population Health \nResearch Centre, Universit\u00e9 de Bordeaux, Bordeaux, France \nemelyne.gres@u-bordeaux.fr.\n(2)Center for Epidemiology and Research in Population Health (CERPOP), UMR 1295, \nInserm, University Paul Sabatier Toulouse 3, Toulouse, France.\n(3)Institut de Nutrition et Sant\u00e9 de l'Enfant, Conakry, Guinea.\n(4)Gabriel Tour\u00e9 University Hospital Center, Bamako, Mali.\n(5)CHU de Bogodogo, Ouagadougou, Burkina Faso.\n(6)Tdh, Terre des hommes, Ouagadougou, Burkina Faso.\n(7)ALIMA, The Alliance for International Medical Action, Bamako, Mali.\n(8)ALIMA, The Alliance for International Medical Action, Conakry, Guinea.\n(9)Solthis, Solidarit\u00e9 Th\u00e9rapeutique et Initiatives pour la Sant\u00e9, Niamey, \nNiger.\n(10)Tdh, Terre des hommes, Dakar, Senegal.\n(11)Solthis, Solidarit\u00e9 Th\u00e9rapeutique et Initiatives pour la Sant\u00e9, Paris, \nFrance.\n(12)Universit\u00e9 Paris Cit\u00e9, IRD, Inserm, Ceped, Paris, France.\n\nOBJECTIVE: To describe antibiotic prescribing practices using the WHO AWaRe \n(Access, Watch, Reserve) classification in West African children under 5 years \nof age attending public primary health centres (PHCs).\nDESIGN: Cross-sectional study.\nSETTING: The AIRE project implemented the systematic use of pulse oximetry into \nintegrated management of childhood illness consultations in West African \ncountries (Burkina Faso, Guinea, Mali and Niger). We described antibiotic \nprescriptions for outpatient children at 16 PHCs and for severe cases referred \nat district hospitals.\nPATIENTS: Between 14 June 2021 and 19 June 2022, 15\u2009854 outpatients were \nincluded: 968 neonates and young infants (0-28 days) and 14\u2009886 children (2-59 \nmonths). Among them, 78 (8.1%) neonates and young infants and 385 (2.6%) \nchildren were hospitalised. We evaluated 58 hospitalised neonates and young \ninfants and 275 hospitalised children, respectively.\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURES: Frequency of antibiotic prescriptions according to the \nAWaRe classification recommended by WHO.\nRESULTS: At the PHC level, proportions of neonates and young infants with \u22651 \nantibiotic prescription were 83%, 62%, 71% and 59% in Burkina Faso, Guinea, Mali \nand Niger, respectively. A total of 805 antibiotics were prescribed (85% Access \nand 13% Watch). The proportions of children with \u22651 antibiotic prescription \nreached 71%, 66%, 63% and 36% in Burkina Faso, Guinea, Mali and Niger, \nrespectively. Out of the 9630 antibiotics prescribed, 93% were Access (mainly \namoxicillin), and 7% Watch. At the hospital level, Watch antibiotics were mainly \nprescribed for severe cases referred. No Reserve antibiotics were prescribed.\nCONCLUSIONS: High proportions of antibiotics were prescribed to outpatient \nchildren included, the appropriateness of which needs further study. \nNevertheless, in every country, the proportion prescribed in the Access group \nreached the minimum threshold of 60% of all antibiotic prescriptions, as \nrecommended by WHO.\nTRIAL REGISTRATION NUMBER: PACTR202206525204526.\n\n\u00a9 Author(s) (or their employer(s)) 2024. Re-use permitted under CC BY-NC. No \ncommercial re-use. See